s: 161\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 18 Aug 2026 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 28 Sep 2026 (this version, v3)]\nL9: # Title:PTXBench: Benchmarking and Adapting LLMs for GPU Kernel Optimization with Architecture-specific PTX\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Genghan Zhang , cite7†Yixin Dong , cite8†Chengze Fan , cite9†Zhichen Zeng , cite10†Yueming Yuan , cite11†Shaowei Zhu , cite12†Kunle Olukotun L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled PTXBench: Benchmarking and Adapting LLMs for GPU Kernel Optimization with Architecture-specific PTX, by Genghan Zhang and 6 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite13†View PDF cite14†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:We introduce PTXBench, a benchmark for evaluating and adapting large language models (LLMs) to use architecture-specific PTX for GPU kernel optimization. PTXBench measures functional correctness, whether selected target instructions execute at runtime, and speedup over frontier libraries across GEMM and attention workloads on H100 and B200 GPUs.\nL17: Our evaluation shows that architecture-specific PTX capability remains uneven: success rates fall substantially on complex attention backward workloads, and executing the target instructions does not necessarily translate into competitive performance. No evaluated model consistently matches frontier libraries across the suite. We further adapt Qwen3.6-27B using supervised fine-tuning.\nL18: Repair-conditioned training improves several tasks, but generalization remains uneven; data coverage, balance, and the quality of the reasoning teacher matter in addition to dataset size. PTXBench provides an auditable testbed for measuring and improving LLMs' ability to exploit evolving GPU architectures.\nL19: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite15†arXiv:2608.17379 [cs.CL]\nL21:    | (or cite16†arXiv:2608.17379v3 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL22:    | cite17†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2608.17379†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Genghan Zhang [cite18†view email ]\nL26: cite5†[v1] Tue, 18 Aug 2026 05:14:41 UTC (5,480 KB)\nL27: cite19†[v2] Wed, 19 Aug 2026 20:36:11 UTC (5,480 KB)\nL28: [v3] Mon, 28 Sep 2026 05:21:04 UTC (7,272 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled PTXBench: Benchmarking and Adapting LLMs for GPU Kernel Optimization with Architecture-specific PTX, by Genghan Zhang and 6 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite13†View PDF L36:   * cite14†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite20†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite21†view license†creativecommons.org L40: \nL41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.CL\nL44: \nL45: cite22†< prev |   cite23†next > L46: \nL47: cite24†new | cite25†recent | cite26†2026-08 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite27†cs L52: cite28†cs.AI L53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite29†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite30†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L57:   * cite31†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L58: \nL59: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL60: \nL61: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL62: \nL63: [Button: ×]\nL64: \nL65: loading...\nL66: \nL67: Data provided by:\nL68: \nL69: ### Bookmark\nL70: \nL71: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL72: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL73: \nL74: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL75: \nL76: Bibliographic Explorer (cite32†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL77: \nL78: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL79: \nL80: Connected Papers (cite33†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com